tance and justification for increasing the AGI's autonomy. Society may learn not only how to supervise the AGI, but also it may learn to trust it.", "doi": "10.3389/frai.2026.1904357", "pmid": "42769121", "pmcid": "PMC13590425", "source": "MED", "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Subscription required", "availabilityCode": "S", "documentStyle": "doi", "site": "DOI", "url": "https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1904357"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "html", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13590425"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "pdf", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13590425?pdf=render"}]}}, {"title": "Agentic genomics: From pipeline automation to autonomous validation.", "abstract": "Genomics has entered a phase in which AI agents can autonomously discover, configure, execute, and chain bioinformatics operations from natural-language instructions. We term this paradigm \"agentic genomics\": the delegation of multi-step genomic analyses to autonomous software agents that select tools, manage dependencies, and adapt execution in response to intermediate results, mediated by large language models (LLMs) and constrained by domain-specific skill libraries. We argue that agentic genomics shifts the bottleneck in computational biology from pipeline construction to validation. We examine emerging systems, including CellAtria, AutoBA, Bio-Copilot, and ClawBio, and assess their divergent architectures. We propose a tiered validation framework spanning research-grade, benchmarked, and clinical-grade analyses and argue that equity-aware design must be a systems requirement rather than an optional aspiration. We identify the infrastructure needed to make agentic genomics trustworthy.", "doi": "10.1016/j.xgen.2026.101305", "pmid": "42480539", "pmcid": "PMC13477014", "source": "MED", "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Subscription required", "availabilityCode": "S", "documentStyle": "doi", "site": "DOI", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xgen.2026.101305"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "html", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13477014"}, {"availability": "Open access", "availabilityCode": "OA", "documentStyle": "pdf", "site": "Europe_PMC", "url": "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13477014?pdf=render"}]}}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "c1d068ef4b942b1a5dbd98fc715da2fd809c8ad121a0ff17fa2ff4066331a943", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "unassigned_problem", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.3390/biology15171537", "doi:10.3389/frai.2026.1904357", "doi:10.1016/j.xgen.2026.101305", "pmc:PMC13565537", "pmc:PMC13590425", "pmc:PMC13477014"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1904357", "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13590425", "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13590425?pdf=render", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xgen.2026.101305", "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13477014", "https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13477014?pdf=render"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}

